Soooo...

After taking a break, going out for 10 minutes and having a drink, I decided to give it one more try before giving up on the coding platform.

I did this:

    def rental_car_cost(days):
        if days >= 7:
            return 40 * days - 50
        elif days >= 3:
            return 40 * days - 20
        else:
            return 40 * days

... and it worked. I feel silly for making things complicated in the first place. Can anyone explain why my initial attempt returned an error? Was it because "cost" wasn't defined or something else?

I think the online learning platform doesn't want me to think outside the box. Or it's slightly bugged.

I have no other source to learn things and practice them at the same time, so I must stick to it until I get enough info and move to something more advanced and not user-friendly.

Basically, the platform teaches you the most basic things using test cases and is beginner-friendly. Until yesterday, I had no idea what an elif statement is and how to use it.

The code I wrote in the original post didn't work, but this one worked:

    def rental_car_cost(days):
        if days >= 7:
            return 40 * days - 50
        elif days >= 3:
            return 40 * days - 20
        else:
            return 40 * days

Is there any real difference between this and what I wrote in the original post? In a real coding scenario, aren't both methods pretty much the same?

Hi,

This cannot be unwanted, any help is appreciated. Thank you for that.

The dictionary idea seems great, but I have a question.

My idea was to create a variable for every rule (I'm going to add about 20 rules, just to get it started), with a description for every variable. Such as:

    crash_rule = "\n&lt;removed&gt;&lt;removed&gt;"

What you are suggesting is that I should add a dictionary like:

    keywords_dictionary = {"crash": "&lt;removed&gt;", "spillout": "&lt;removed&gt;", "next rule": "&lt;removed&gt;"}

When the user inputs the keyword crash, what he should see on his screen is something similar to this:
&gt; &lt;removed&gt;

&gt; &lt;removed&gt;

&gt; &lt;removed&gt;

Would this work? Then, the tricky part comes when if and elifs come into play, but I'm just making the lists for now. 
Edit: The dict above might not be written correctly.

So... I jumped out of my chair when I saw it working:

    keywords_dict = {"crash": "&lt;removed&gt;", "something else": "1234"}
    keyword_or_help = input ("\nDescribe your issue in 1 word, or type help for a list of suggestions: ")
    if keyword_or_help in keywords_dict:
        print (keywords_dict[keyword_or_help])
    else:
        print ("Sorry, invalid entry. Please type help for a list of suggestions")

I went through several syntax errors before I figured out that I can ask the programming language to print the keyword_or_help "input variable" if it's in the dictionary. This is so much easier than several statements inside statements. A large tech company didn't help with the print (keywords_dict[keyword_or_help]) but I'm glad I figured it out myself.

It feels so satisfying, hah. :D 
Looks as intended: &lt;removed&gt;

Edit: After this, I'll create some kind of loop (will look it up) to ask if the rule is right, if not, keyword_or_help pops again, asks for another keyword, brings another rule and so on until the rule is the one the user is looking for. Should be a fun and looooooooooooong weekend.
